Has anyone heard @danasalah’s new song Weino? 🎵 

The entire 2 min and 25 seconds had my falaha heart beaming with pride. And rightfully so as she’s part of an entirely new genre of music (coined “Falahe Pop” by the brilliant @SuhelNafar) just for peeps like me to feel seen (and for peeps like you to enjoy). 

For those who don’t know “falaha/falahe” is an Arabic word that literally translates into “farmer”. It’s the term for Palestinians from rural areas of Palestine (aka villagers!). We have our own traditions and dialect (every time I don’t say “chnafeh” I know my ancestors are pained 😩). Because Arab culture is mainly patriarchal, children take after the dads status - making me a proud falaha ✊🏽🇵🇸. 

This part of the song in particular gave me intergenerational flashbacks (is that a thing?) and I immediately thought of this gorgeous necklace my mother in law @wafaasad61 gifted me for my birthday a few years ago. Authentic Palestinian silver. Heavy as hell - because it carries all the stories of ancestral past. 

Shout out to all of the INCREDIBLE Palestinian musicians making waves in the music industry right now. Thank you for representing our people with your beautiful sounds and talent. 🤲🏽 

Some of my faves for you to follow: 
@zachmatari (obvi 🤪)
@danasalah 
@basharmuradofficial 
@aneestherapper 

#falaha #weino #falahaqueen

Part 3️⃣: The Modernization of Tatreez 

#Thobes are still worn today as daily clothing, mainly by elderly Palestinian matriarchs. They are commonly worn for celebrations like Hennas and weddings by Palestinian women and girls of all ages as a nod to more traditional past times. 

Our younger generations have gotten creative. Transferring these ancient designs and unique cross-stitching onto modern clothing and accessories (like denim jackets, bags, skirts & shirts, hoodies, masks ~thank you COVID~, etc.). All of which create new ways of keeping Palestinian heritage alive and giving us subtle ways of protesting our people’s brutal colonization. 

There is something so enchanting about #Tatreez. Almost like the mind immediately knows that there is a story hidden within the stitches it’s looking at. 

I can never get enough it - and I am so unbelievably grateful to those who have developed the talent to keep this traditional art form alive, to keep our culture alive in such a beautiful way. 

📸: @worldviabelal 

ACCOUNTS TO FOLLOW (my favorites who modernize Tatreez): 
@ramikashou
@suzytamimi
@anat.international 
@baladystitch

Shout out to all the Palestinian youth & owned brands for modernizing our signature designs so that we can wear our heritage on our sleeves (literally) and subtly protest everywhere we go. 

I used to think #Tatreez was weird until I - say it with me now - “decolonized fashion standards”. Tribal print is beautiful. Indigenous fashion is beautiful. And it’s necessary that we support those keeping it alive. 

I never understood the importance or meaning behind “shop local”, until I learned how shopping local/small directly impacts marginalized communities and helps us thrive. I do my best to purchase at least one piece from a Palestinian brand I’m newly introduced to. 

Here are some of my favorite small biz/local Palestinian owned brands keeping our history and culture alive that I hope you’ll support: 

@fashionsaru
@paliroots
@baladystitch
@rou_nyc
@darzahdesigns 
@sawa_designs 

And because I know you’ll ask - the fitted maxi skirt was an old Palestinian #Thobe repurposed into this amazing piece and given to my grandmother in the 1960s 🥰 (funny story, she apparently traded her comforter for it 😆). Grateful to have this piece in my closet. 

